import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { CV_URL } from '../consts';
---

<PageLayout
	title="About | Itziar Morales"
	description="Software Engineering student in Madrid — systems programming, backend, and data engineering. Also games, fantasy novels, and a home server."
>
	<p class="eyebrow">~/about</p>
	<h1>About</h1>

	<p>
		Hi, I'm Itziar, a Software Engineering student at <a href="https://www.u-tad.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">U-tad</a> (Madrid)
		with a minor in Data Science &amp; AI, expected to graduate in June 2027. I started programming at 18
		by enrolling at <a href="https://www.digipen.es" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DigiPen Bilbao</a> for
		a B.S. in Computer Science in Real Time Interactive Simulation, then pivoted to Computational Mathematics,
		and eventually landed on Software Engineering. That path wasn't the most direct, but it gave me a genuinely
		wide base to work from.
	</p>

	<p>
		Because of it, I've touched most corners of the field &mdash; systems programming in C++, backend
		services, databases of every persuasion, and the Python data/ML stack. The full list, honestly
		self-rated, lives in <a href="/stack">~/stack</a>.
	</p>

	<p>
		Mathematics has been the constant thread: linear algebra for rendering pipelines, kinematics for
		collision detection, discrete maths for graph traversal, statistics for ML. Three years of
		Computational Mathematics turned that instinct into a foundation &mdash; I think about convergence
		and numerical stability as naturally as I think about runtime complexity.
	</p>

	<p>
		Outside of code I have a vocational degree in music (violoncello) and ABRSM Grade 8 in both piano
		and cello, which led to me composing the soundtrack for a college game project. I use Linux as my
		daily driver and keep a server running Nextcloud, Navidrome, and BookLore, and I tinker with
		dotfiles for window managers and Neovim more than I probably should. I also spent time as a video editor
		and content collaborator for Paradox Interactive. I think long-term disciplined practice, whether in
		music or engineering, builds the kind of rigour that shows up in production-quality work.
	</p>

	<p>
		The rest of my free time splits fairly evenly between games and books. I bounce between retro
		titles and modern releases depending on the mood, and the games I've built have left me with a
		soft spot for good pixel art &mdash; plus a small pile of Python scripts for slicing tilesets
		and bundling spritesheets, because wrangling those assets by hand gets old fast. I read a lot of
		fantasy fiction, and I build my own PCs (and the family's, and the server's) &mdash; picking parts
		is half the fun.
	</p>

	<p>
		On the language side, I grew up attending a British school, and later spent several years at a language
		academy specifically to sit the Cambridge CPE exam and achieve a C2 CEFR certification. Spanish is my
		native language, and I also have an intermediate level of French. I love languages not just for the
		doors they open, but because each one gives you a different lens on the world, and I find that
		genuinely enriching.
	</p>

	<p>
		I'm currently interning as a Data Analytics Intern at MGA (remote), where most of my time goes
		to the data-engineering side of a digital transformation: designing relational schemas, cleaning
		and migrating years of spreadsheet data into PostgreSQL, and building Spring Boot services on top.
		The part I enjoy most is turning implicit business rules &mdash; the kind that live in people's
		heads and formula cells &mdash; into constraints the database enforces itself.
	</p>

	<p>
		There's a <a href={CV_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">CV</a> if you want the concise version
		&mdash; and if any of this overlaps with something you're building, or you just have strong opinions
		about fantasy novels or window managers, <a href="mailto:itzimr97@pm.me">get in touch</a>.
	</p>
</PageLayout>
